# Your Experience — used by upwork-pitch-page and upwork-proposal

This is your own track record, in one place, so both skills draw on the same real facts instead
of each inventing its own version. Copy this file to `context/experience.md` and replace every
`[PLACEHOLDER]` — nothing in it does anything until you do. No fixed length; a few honest lines
beat a padded section, and a section left empty renders an honest "not added yet" note wherever
it's used rather than being silently skipped or filled with a guess.

Two kinds of section here. Some are free text that `upwork-proposal` reads loosely, pulling
whatever's relevant into a proposal. Others (marked below) have a small fixed shape, because
`upwork-pitch-page` passes their lines straight through as `generate.py` flags — keep to the shape
so nothing silently drops.

## What you do

`[PLACEHOLDER — free text.]` Your core skills, the tools/platforms you work in, the type of
client and project you're good for. `upwork-proposal` draws on this to connect your background to
a specific job without inventing anything.

## Track record

**Fixed shape:** one line per number, `value | label`. These become `upwork-pitch-page`'s
`--stat` flags — the CV's headline numbers. Real, verifiable numbers only; an empty list here
means the pitch page's background section shows an honest "not added yet" line instead of
numbers that aren't true.

- `[PLACEHOLDER]` — e.g. `$10K+ | Earned on Upwork`
- `[PLACEHOLDER]` — e.g. `15 | Projects delivered`
- `[PLACEHOLDER]` — e.g. `82% | Job Success Score`

## What clients say

**Fixed shape:** one short trait per line — real, client-sourced praise (Upwork's own "Insights
from completed jobs" tags if you have them, or phrases you've genuinely heard repeated), not
self-description. Becomes `--trait` flags.

- `[PLACEHOLDER]` — e.g. `Collaborative`
- `[PLACEHOLDER]` — e.g. `Clear Communicator`

## Notable clients / past projects

`[PLACEHOLDER — free text.]` Company or project names you can name publicly, plus an optional
closing note about scale ("...plus 15+ smaller builds across GoHighLevel and n8n, from solo
coaches to teams of 50"). Becomes `--client` (one per name) and `--client-note` (the closing
clause).

## Background

**Fixed shape:** one line per item, `role | institution`. Employment and education both go here,
most relevant first. Becomes `--background` flags.

- `[PLACEHOLDER]` — e.g. `MBA, Marketing & AI | Your University`
- `[PLACEHOLDER]` — e.g. `Sales & Marketing | Your Previous Company`

## Languages

`[PLACEHOLDER]` — e.g. `English (native), German (fluent), French (basic)`. One line, becomes
`--languages`.

## Portfolio links

`[PLACEHOLDER — free text.]` Your Upwork profile URL (required by `upwork-pitch-page`'s
`--profile-url`), plus anything optional: a YouTube channel or portfolio site (`--youtube-url`),
your own site or a second product (`--site-url`), a public workspace with more diagrams you've
mapped for other clients (`--proof-link-url`). Leave the optional ones out entirely if you don't
have them — the pitch page omits or honestly placeholders whatever's missing rather than pointing
at a broken or made-up link.

## Tone & unique selling points

`[PLACEHOLDER — free text.]` What makes you different, how you like to sound when pitching
yourself. `upwork-proposal` uses this to keep the proposal's voice consistently yours across jobs.
